  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "Start the rice: rinse it, cook it with 1 1/2 cups water, covered, over the lowest heat for 15 minutes, then leave the lid on off the heat.",
      "timer_min": 15
    },
    {
      "text": "Season the shrimp with the lime juice, half the salt, and the chili flakes while you slice the vegetables."
    },
    {
      "text": "Warm the palm oil in the braising pan over medium heat — it will turn everything sunset-orange. Cook the onion and peppers with the cilantro stems for 5 minutes, until they slump.",
      "timer_min": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Add the garlic for 30 seconds, then the tomato slices and the rest of the salt. Cook 3 minutes until the tomatoes relax into a rough sauce.",
      "timer_min": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Pour in the coconut milk and bring to a lazy simmer — bubbles at the edges, not a boil, or the coconut milk can split."
    },
    {
      "text": "Lay the shrimp and their lime marinade over the sauce. Simmer 5-6 minutes, turning once, until the shrimp curl into loose Cs and the flesh turns opaque; shellfish are safe at 145°F (63°C) on a thermometer.",
      "timer_min": 6,
      "safety": {
        "class": "flesh_shellfish",
        "temp_f": 145,
        "method": "thermometer",
        "source": "USDA FSIS safe minimum internal temperature table"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Taste and add the last of the lime. Shower with the cilantro leaves and carry the pan to the table over rice."
    }
  ],
  "cuisine_note": "Bahian moqueca is built in layers in one pan — vegetables, then seafood, then coconut milk — and never stirred into mush; the dendê (red palm oil) gives the dish its color and its low, warm hum of a street stall in Salvador.",
